p.src:
Return now to a phase space with canonical coordinates $(q_1,\ldots,q_N,p_1,\ldots,p_N)$. Hamilton’s equations are $\dot q_i=\frac{\partial H}{\partial p_i},
 \qquad
 \dot p_i=-\frac{\partial H}{\partial q_i}.$ They define a phase-space velocity field $\bm u_H=(\dot q_1,\ldots,\dot q_N,
          \dot p_1,\ldots,\dot p_N).$ Its divergence is $\begin{aligned}
 \bm{\nabla}_{q,p}\!\cdot\bm u_H
 &=\sum_{i=1}^{N}
 \left(
  \frac{\partial\dot q_i}{\partial q_i}
  +\frac{\partial\dot p_i}{\partial p_i}
 \right) \notag\\
 &=\sum_{i=1}^{N}
 \left(
  \frac{\partial^2 H}{\partial q_i\partial p_i}
  -\frac{\partial^2 H}{\partial p_i\partial q_i}
 \right)=0.
 \end{aligned}$ Each pair of mixed derivatives cancels. This local identity is the whole proof.

