p.src:
As a final check, Hamilton’s equations give $\dot x_i=\frac{\partial H}{\partial p_i}
 =\frac{p_i-qA_i}{m}=v_i,$ and $\dot p_i=-\frac{\partial H}{\partial x_i}
 =qv_j\frac{\partial A_j}{\partial x_i}.$ Differentiating the mechanical momentum gives $\begin{aligned}
 m\dot v_i
 &=\dot p_i-q\frac{dA_i}{dt}\notag\\
 &=qv_j\left(
   \frac{\partial A_j}{\partial x_i}
  -\frac{\partial A_i}{\partial x_j}
  \right)
 =q(\bm v\times\bm B)_i.
 \end{aligned}$ Thus Newtonian, Lagrangian, and Hamiltonian descriptions all produce the same magnetic motion. The vector potential changes the canonical bookkeeping, but the observable force depends only on its curl.

